# ouroboros-engine — what the production image's build context may contain.
#
# The context is this directory (see Dockerfile), so this is the file BuildKit reads for
# it — a `Dockerfile.dockerignore` would be read by nothing here, and a root
# `.dockerignore` would apply to every image in the repository. ../docs/CONVENTIONS.md
# § 5 names this module as one of the three that build from their own directory: nothing
# in this image installs through the workspace lockfile at the root.
#
# An allow-list rather than a deny-list: `*` first, then the files the build reads. A
# deny-list has to be extended every time something is added to this directory, and the
# first thing it would let through is `.env` — a real shared secret, in a layer, in a
# published image.
#
# Excluded by the `*` and worth naming for the reader: `.env` and `.env.example` (a
# container is configured by its environment — Dockerfile, runtime stage), `.venv/` (a
# host-resolved environment, and the image builds its own), `tests/` (they belong to CI,
# not to a production image), `package.json` (the workspace adapter, which is `yarn dev`'s
# way in and not this image's), and the caches `pytest` and `ruff` leave behind.

*

# The manifest and the lockfile — what `uv sync --locked` resolves and installs from.
!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock

# The package itself. Installed as a wheel rather than copied into the runtime, so this
# is read by the build stage alone.
!src/

# Declared as the project's readme in pyproject.toml, which makes it a file the wheel
# build fails without rather than prose the image happens to carry.
!README.md

# The API specification. Both files are force-included into the package by the wheel
# build: the JSON is what the service loads and serves at /openapi.json, and the YAML is
# the authoritative document it was rendered from.
!openapi.yaml
!openapi.json
